1
00:00:00,566 --> 00:00:03,608
Imagine you are writing a program for a Rubik's cube.

2
00:00:03,982 --> 00:00:08,186
Before it can find a solution, it needs to do something much more basic.

3
00:00:08,546 --> 00:00:10,337
Turn the top layer correctly.

4
00:00:12,766 --> 00:00:13,366
Watch.

5
00:00:13,740 --> 00:00:18,119
The white face turns, but strips of stickers on the sides move too.

6
00:00:18,493 --> 00:00:19,776
Which colors go where?

7
00:00:22,606 --> 00:00:26,215
You could record each sticker and write rules for moving it.

8
00:00:26,589 --> 00:00:27,363
That works.

9
00:00:27,737 --> 00:00:30,415
But the real cube never consults a list.

10
00:00:30,775 --> 00:00:33,133
Its shape tells the colors how to move.

11
00:00:35,354 --> 00:00:40,610
Can we give our program a description that makes those same rules follow from the geometry?

12
00:00:40,987 --> 00:00:42,824
That is the problem we will solve.

13
00:00:46,525 --> 00:00:48,970
Start with one small colored square.

14
00:00:49,344 --> 00:00:51,703
We will call it a sticker, or facelet.

15
00:00:52,062 --> 00:00:54,697
Nine stickers make up one large face.

16
00:00:55,071 --> 00:00:58,432
There are six faces, and fifty four stickers in all.

17
00:01:00,361 --> 00:01:02,501
A face is an outside surface.

18
00:01:02,875 --> 00:01:06,135
The objects that carry those stickers live underneath it.

19
00:01:06,494 --> 00:01:07,370
Let's look at one.

20
00:01:10,304 --> 00:01:12,255
Here is a natural first attempt.

21
00:01:12,615 --> 00:01:17,444
Number the fifty four sticker positions, and store their colors in a list.

22
00:01:17,818 --> 00:01:21,078
This is a perfectly valid way to represent the cube.

23
00:01:23,407 --> 00:01:24,486
Now turn the top.

24
00:01:24,846 --> 00:01:29,181
Each moving sticker has to jump from its old list entry to a new one.

25
00:01:29,598 --> 00:01:31,056
Follow the colored paths.

26
00:01:31,415 --> 00:01:36,288
A single smooth turn has become a collection of scattered index changes.

27
00:01:39,527 --> 00:01:43,368
We could specify those permutations, one move at a time.

28
00:01:43,742 --> 00:01:49,196
But the list order gives us little help in seeing why these stickers belong to the same turn.

29
00:01:49,556 --> 00:01:52,306
Can we keep the spatial structure in our description?

30
00:01:56,191 --> 00:01:58,244
This little block is a cubelet.

31
00:01:58,604 --> 00:02:02,111
It sits on an edge, between two large faces.

32
00:02:02,470 --> 00:02:05,672
That is why it has two stickers: green and white.

33
00:02:07,618 --> 00:02:10,659
The stickers are attached to the same rigid object.

34
00:02:11,034 --> 00:02:14,802
However we turn it, they stay together, at a right angle.

35
00:02:15,161 --> 00:02:18,872
Our description should preserve that relationship automatically.

36
00:02:22,289 --> 00:02:23,543
Put that cubelet back.

37
00:02:23,917 --> 00:02:30,084
When we say turn the top face, we physically rotate the layer of nine cubelets behind it.

38
00:02:30,443 --> 00:02:33,005
This layer is also called an outer slice.

39
00:02:34,949 --> 00:02:37,802
Watch the highlighted layer make a quarter turn.

40
00:02:38,161 --> 00:02:40,549
The other eighteen positions stay put.

41
00:02:40,923 --> 00:02:45,098
The colored squares on the sides move because their cubelets move.

42
00:02:48,019 --> 00:02:49,476
So we need two rules.

43
00:02:49,835 --> 00:02:50,740
Choose a layer.

44
00:02:51,115 --> 00:02:53,458
Then rotate everything in it together.

45
00:02:56,872 --> 00:03:00,176
The cube suggests a better starting point for our purpose.

46
00:03:00,535 --> 00:03:04,536
Arrange its small blocks in a three by three by three array.

47
00:03:04,910 --> 00:03:08,940
One slot per cubelet position, with its stickers attached.

48
00:03:09,300 --> 00:03:12,472
Now the top layer is an entire plane of this grid.

49
00:03:14,790 --> 00:03:17,555
That makes it easier to see what belongs together.

50
00:03:17,915 --> 00:03:23,151
But we still need a rule for moving those blocks and changing which way their stickers face.

51
00:03:25,478 --> 00:03:30,131
Is there a simple language that describes a turn of this three dimensional array?

52
00:03:30,508 --> 00:03:35,176
A language where the motion of the colors follows from the motion of the blocks?

53
00:03:39,362 --> 00:03:41,153
Follow our edge through that turn.

54
00:03:41,513 --> 00:03:42,738
Where it sits changes.

55
00:03:43,112 --> 00:03:45,630
Which way its green sticker points changes too.

56
00:03:45,990 --> 00:03:50,092
Knowing only its position would not tell us where to draw its colors.

57
00:03:52,007 --> 00:03:54,423
We need both location and facing.

58
00:03:54,783 --> 00:04:00,310
And because the stickers are rigidly attached, the same physical turn changes both.

59
00:04:00,669 --> 00:04:03,711
Could one mathematical operation do both jobs?

60
00:04:07,666 --> 00:04:10,315
First we will give each cubelet an address.

61
00:04:10,689 --> 00:04:13,862
Then we will see what that address tells us about its stickers.

62
00:04:14,294 --> 00:04:17,205
Finally, we will put the description into motion.

63
00:04:19,116 --> 00:04:20,864
No matrix is needed yet.

64
00:04:21,296 --> 00:04:24,541
First, let's make the geometry feel familiar.

65
00:04:27,441 --> 00:04:29,582
We need a way to describe where things are.

66
00:04:29,941 --> 00:04:32,968
But what can we use as a reference while the cubelets move?

67
00:04:33,313 --> 00:04:34,640
Look at the six centers.

68
00:04:36,549 --> 00:04:37,963
Turn the top layer again.

69
00:04:38,323 --> 00:04:40,041
Its center spins in place.

70
00:04:40,416 --> 00:04:42,323
The other centers stay where they were.

71
00:04:42,683 --> 00:04:46,393
Outer-layer turns never exchange these six positions.

72
00:04:49,207 --> 00:04:53,963
Imagine a cross joining the opposite centers through the middle of the puzzle.

73
00:04:54,338 --> 00:04:57,990
Those fixed directions give us a frame for every move.

74
00:04:58,364 --> 00:05:02,844
We keep the whole cube in this frame, instead of turning it in our hands.

75
00:05:05,262 --> 00:05:07,184
Put zero at the middle of the puzzle.

76
00:05:07,559 --> 00:05:12,867
We will call the direction toward green x, toward red y, and toward white z.

77
00:05:14,797 --> 00:05:17,446
One step toward green is positive x.

78
00:05:17,820 --> 00:05:21,588
One step the other way, toward blue, is negative x.

79
00:05:21,992 --> 00:05:25,280
Zero means we have not moved along that direction.

80
00:05:25,654 --> 00:05:27,984
The other two axes work the same way.

81
00:05:30,405 --> 00:05:31,848
These names are a choice.

82
00:05:32,207 --> 00:05:35,888
What matters is using the same fixed directions throughout.

83
00:05:38,275 --> 00:05:40,342
Let's locate our edge from the middle.

84
00:05:40,760 --> 00:05:43,235
First, take one step toward green.

85
00:05:43,609 --> 00:05:45,807
That is the x coordinate: one.

86
00:05:47,719 --> 00:05:50,426
Next, take zero steps toward red.

87
00:05:50,800 --> 00:05:52,795
The y coordinate is zero.

88
00:05:53,227 --> 00:05:55,949
Finally, take one step up toward white.

89
00:05:56,309 --> 00:05:58,173
The z coordinate is one.

90
00:06:01,091 --> 00:06:02,722
One, zero, one.

91
00:06:03,155 --> 00:06:07,373
Three numbers, always in x, y, z order.

92
00:06:07,733 --> 00:06:10,309
Together they name exactly this position.

93
00:06:10,669 --> 00:06:14,743
You can read a coordinate as a little set of walking instructions.

94
00:06:17,816 --> 00:06:23,256
Each direction has only three levels: minus one, zero, and one.

95
00:06:23,630 --> 00:06:28,983
The same three numbers locate every block in a three by three by three grid.

96
00:06:30,923 --> 00:06:33,165
That gives twenty seven positions.

97
00:06:33,525 --> 00:06:40,040
This is an idealized geometric model of the puzzle, not a diagram of its internal mechanism.

98
00:06:40,414 --> 00:06:42,496
The middle position has no stickers.

99
00:06:42,856 --> 00:06:45,200
Rubix stores the other twenty six.

100
00:06:47,608 --> 00:06:50,766
Now draw one arrow from the middle to our edge.

101
00:06:51,125 --> 00:06:56,042
Its sideways and upward components are exactly those walking instructions.

102
00:06:56,402 --> 00:07:00,548
This is a vector: an arrow described by three numbers.

103
00:07:02,476 --> 00:07:04,936
We can write the numbers vertically as a column.

104
00:07:05,310 --> 00:07:10,081
The first row is x, the second is y, the third is z.

105
00:07:10,441 --> 00:07:15,256
It means the same thing as one, zero, one written across the page.

106
00:07:17,190 --> 00:07:18,183
Why draw an arrow?

107
00:07:18,542 --> 00:07:20,653
Because an arrow can turn with the cube.

108
00:07:21,028 --> 00:07:23,938
Soon that will let the geometry do our bookkeeping.

109
00:07:27,233 --> 00:07:32,222
This address describes where the green and white edge belongs in the solved cube.

110
00:07:32,582 --> 00:07:34,010
Call that home vector c.

111
00:07:35,933 --> 00:07:38,771
Keep c fixed, even after a scramble.

112
00:07:39,131 --> 00:07:42,114
It names the same cubelet throughout the story.

113
00:07:42,474 --> 00:07:46,169
Later we will use a different vector for where that cubelet is now.

114
00:07:49,275 --> 00:07:50,746
Look at a center cubelet.

115
00:07:51,106 --> 00:07:54,162
Its home address has one nonzero coordinate.

116
00:07:54,536 --> 00:07:58,290
It touches one outside boundary, and carries one sticker.

117
00:08:00,192 --> 00:08:02,827
An edge has two nonzero coordinates.

118
00:08:03,201 --> 00:08:04,702
Two outside boundaries.

119
00:08:05,076 --> 00:08:05,966
Two stickers.

120
00:08:06,326 --> 00:08:10,428
A corner has three: three boundaries, three stickers.

121
00:08:12,857 --> 00:08:14,269
This is our first payoff.

122
00:08:14,607 --> 00:08:18,339
A coordinate of zero stays in the middle along that axis.

123
00:08:18,677 --> 00:08:22,658
A coordinate of plus or minus one reaches an outside surface.

124
00:08:22,996 --> 00:08:26,186
Counting the nonzero coordinates counts the stickers!

125
00:08:29,708 --> 00:08:35,482
For our edge, one, zero, one gives one plus zero plus one.

126
00:08:35,856 --> 00:08:36,746
Two stickers.

127
00:08:37,106 --> 00:08:41,993
Now consider the opposite edge, minus one, zero, minus one.

128
00:08:42,353 --> 00:08:43,810
It still has two stickers.

129
00:08:45,756 --> 00:08:50,949
The minus signs choose the opposite faces; they do not subtract stickers.

130
00:08:51,323 --> 00:08:53,638
Take absolute values, then add.

131
00:08:53,997 --> 00:08:58,841
That little formula counts the exposed surfaces of every cubelet.

132
00:09:01,754 --> 00:09:05,754
Our choice of address has already given us something useful for free.

133
00:09:06,114 --> 00:09:10,769
Now ask the next question: can it also tell us which colors those stickers have?

134
00:09:14,456 --> 00:09:19,489
The green center sits one step along x: one, zero, zero.

135
00:09:19,863 --> 00:09:24,939
The white center sits one step along z: zero, zero, one.

136
00:09:25,313 --> 00:09:31,015
Blue is the opposite of green, so its vector is minus one, zero, zero.

137
00:09:32,948 --> 00:09:36,353
These are unit vectors: arrows one step long.

138
00:09:36,712 --> 00:09:40,873
We can use each center's fixed vector as the name of its color.

139
00:09:41,233 --> 00:09:46,672
Green means this direction at home, even if a green sticker later points somewhere else.

140
00:09:49,508 --> 00:09:53,421
The short name for the positive x unit vector is e x.

141
00:09:53,534 --> 00:09:56,299
Likewise e y and e z.

142
00:09:56,674 --> 00:10:00,500
The letter e just names a one-step arrow along an axis.

143
00:10:02,900 --> 00:10:04,197
Look at our edge at home.

144
00:10:04,557 --> 00:10:07,278
Draw an arrow straight out of the green sticker.

145
00:10:07,638 --> 00:10:09,459
It points toward the green center.

146
00:10:09,833 --> 00:10:14,299
Do the same for white: that arrow points up, toward the white center.

147
00:10:16,693 --> 00:10:20,389
An arrow perpendicular to a surface is called a normal.

148
00:10:20,748 --> 00:10:23,586
These normals tell us which way the stickers face.

149
00:10:23,946 --> 00:10:29,328
At home, they are exactly the unit vectors we just used to name green and white.

150
00:10:32,733 --> 00:10:36,894
Now go back to our home vector: one, zero, one.

151
00:10:37,253 --> 00:10:40,774
Split its walking instructions into separate arrows.

152
00:10:41,134 --> 00:10:42,577
One green x arrow.

153
00:10:42,951 --> 00:10:43,841
No y arrow.

154
00:10:44,201 --> 00:10:45,818
One white z arrow.

155
00:10:48,651 --> 00:10:50,972
There they are: the two sticker normals.

156
00:10:51,314 --> 00:10:56,502
The components of the address point straight out through the cubelet's colored surfaces!

157
00:10:59,701 --> 00:11:02,176
This is why the coordinate choice matters.

158
00:11:02,550 --> 00:11:05,068
The numbers do not just locate the block.

159
00:11:05,428 --> 00:11:10,388
Each nonzero component also identifies a sticker and its home direction.

160
00:11:13,497 --> 00:11:17,353
We want to keep those arrows separate, so we can follow each sticker.

161
00:11:17,713 --> 00:11:22,208
Write the green arrow as a column: one, zero, zero.

162
00:11:22,567 --> 00:11:25,362
Beside it, leave an empty column for y.

163
00:11:25,721 --> 00:11:29,795
Then write the white arrow: zero, zero, one.

164
00:11:31,711 --> 00:11:35,116
A rectangular arrangement of numbers is a matrix.

165
00:11:35,505 --> 00:11:38,837
Here it is simply a collection of three column vectors.

166
00:11:39,196 --> 00:11:40,813
Read one column at a time.

167
00:11:41,173 --> 00:11:46,657
Each column has x, y, and z entries, just like our address.

168
00:11:49,585 --> 00:11:51,915
There is no new geometry in the grid.

169
00:11:52,274 --> 00:11:54,735
It is a way to keep the arrows side by side.

170
00:11:57,650 --> 00:12:01,679
Notice where the nonzero numbers landed: on the diagonal.

171
00:12:02,039 --> 00:12:03,671
One, zero, one.

172
00:12:04,045 --> 00:12:06,127
The same numbers as our home address!

173
00:12:08,754 --> 00:12:13,554
We call this the diagonal matrix of c, written diag of c.

174
00:12:13,913 --> 00:12:20,938
It is a simple operation: put the three coordinates on the diagonal, and fill the other entries with zeros.

175
00:12:23,554 --> 00:12:26,130
A nonzero column is a sticker normal.

176
00:12:26,490 --> 00:12:30,025
A zero column says there is no sticker on that axis.

177
00:12:30,400 --> 00:12:34,764
Centers, edges, and corners all fit the same rule.

178
00:12:38,483 --> 00:12:39,737
Let's collect what we know.

179
00:12:40,096 --> 00:12:42,804
The home address identifies a cubelet.

180
00:12:43,163 --> 00:12:46,001
Its nonzero coordinates count its stickers.

181
00:12:46,361 --> 00:12:51,757
Split that address into axis arrows, and we get the stickers' home directions.

182
00:12:54,387 --> 00:12:56,004
We have the static description.

183
00:12:56,364 --> 00:12:59,652
To answer our opening problem, we still need to move it.

184
00:13:00,012 --> 00:13:02,632
What happens to an arrow when the cube turns?

185
00:13:06,331 --> 00:13:08,166
Take the one-step x arrow.

186
00:13:08,526 --> 00:13:10,055
Watch it through our top turn.

187
00:13:10,415 --> 00:13:12,483
It ends up pointing toward minus y.

188
00:13:12,857 --> 00:13:15,637
The one-step y arrow turns toward x.

189
00:13:15,997 --> 00:13:18,108
The upward z arrow stays up.

190
00:13:20,032 --> 00:13:24,789
Now think of our edge address as one x arrow plus one z arrow.

191
00:13:25,149 --> 00:13:27,289
Turn both arrows, and add them.

192
00:13:27,649 --> 00:13:29,382
We get minus y plus z.

193
00:13:29,742 --> 00:13:31,984
That is the new location of the edge.

194
00:13:34,992 --> 00:13:37,714
A rigid rotation preserves this addition.

195
00:13:38,074 --> 00:13:43,455
We can turn the ingredients and then add them, or add them first and turn the result.

196
00:13:43,830 --> 00:13:47,336
This is the property that makes linear algebra useful here.

197
00:13:51,247 --> 00:13:53,083
How do we record that rotation?

198
00:13:53,442 --> 00:13:57,632
Keep the destinations of the three one-step arrows as columns.

199
00:13:58,006 --> 00:14:00,205
The first column says where x went.

200
00:14:00,579 --> 00:14:02,414
The second says where y went.

201
00:14:02,788 --> 00:14:04,536
The third says where z went.

202
00:14:07,561 --> 00:14:09,469
This is a rotation matrix.

203
00:14:09,828 --> 00:14:11,140
Call this turn M.

204
00:14:11,398 --> 00:14:18,277
Multiplying it by a vector means: take the indicated amount of each destination arrow, and add.

205
00:14:18,724 --> 00:14:23,858
For one, zero, one, take the first column plus the third.

206
00:14:26,896 --> 00:14:29,997
The grid now has a job: it describes a motion.

207
00:14:30,330 --> 00:14:32,401
We have already seen that motion happen.

208
00:14:35,651 --> 00:14:38,815
We will store the accumulated rotation as R.

209
00:14:39,180 --> 00:14:42,903
At the start, it leaves every arrow unchanged.

210
00:14:43,283 --> 00:14:48,653
This is called the identity matrix: one on the diagonal, zeros elsewhere.

211
00:14:51,387 --> 00:14:55,953
Apply R to the home vector c, and you get the current position, p.

212
00:14:56,302 --> 00:15:00,768
After our turn, R carries that same home address to the new location.

213
00:15:01,117 --> 00:15:03,808
Now use the very same motion on its stickers.

214
00:15:06,411 --> 00:15:10,182
The sticker normals are the columns of our diagonal matrix.

215
00:15:10,530 --> 00:15:14,017
Multiplication transforms each column separately.

216
00:15:14,366 --> 00:15:16,744
Call the collection of current normals N.

217
00:15:18,852 --> 00:15:19,626
Rotate them.

218
00:15:19,985 --> 00:15:22,140
Green now points toward minus y.

219
00:15:22,427 --> 00:15:23,695
White still points up.

220
00:15:24,070 --> 00:15:25,847
The empty column stays empty.

221
00:15:27,958 --> 00:15:32,094
One multiplication gives every sticker direction at once!

222
00:15:34,648 --> 00:15:35,673
Now pause here.

223
00:15:36,028 --> 00:15:39,740
This is the connection that ties the whole encoding together.

224
00:15:41,850 --> 00:15:44,846
Add the home sticker normals, and you get c.

225
00:15:45,314 --> 00:15:49,651
Rotate those normals, then add them, and you get R times c.

226
00:15:51,789 --> 00:15:53,344
That is the cubelet's position.

227
00:15:55,757 --> 00:15:59,769
So the directions of its stickers already determine where it sits.

228
00:16:00,118 --> 00:16:03,888
One rotation keeps location and facing in agreement.

229
00:16:04,251 --> 00:16:05,607
That is the real payoff.

230
00:16:08,275 --> 00:16:09,892
We can rotate a cubelet.

231
00:16:10,251 --> 00:16:12,043
Now choose which ones should move.

232
00:16:12,402 --> 00:16:15,924
Remember our first layer: the nine cubelets at the top.

233
00:16:16,298 --> 00:16:18,772
Their current z coordinate is one.

234
00:16:19,147 --> 00:16:22,799
The middle layer has zero; the bottom has minus one.

235
00:16:25,427 --> 00:16:29,645
Let v be the one-step arrow pointing out of the face we want to turn.

236
00:16:30,020 --> 00:16:36,317
The dot product of v with the current position reads how far that cubelet lies in this direction.

237
00:16:36,691 --> 00:16:38,875
For the top, it simply reads z.

238
00:16:41,806 --> 00:16:43,808
Positive selects the outer layer.

239
00:16:44,155 --> 00:16:48,739
The same test works for every face, including the negative directions.

240
00:16:49,086 --> 00:16:52,956
One geometric rule chooses the nine cubelets to turn.

241
00:16:56,384 --> 00:16:58,450
Here is a useful check on our story.

242
00:16:58,798 --> 00:17:03,208
Our edge started on the front, but the top turn carried it to the left.

243
00:17:05,329 --> 00:17:07,589
Its home address still identifies it.

244
00:17:07,944 --> 00:17:12,762
To choose the moving layer, use its current address: R times c.

245
00:17:14,904 --> 00:17:18,674
The two jobs are different, and the notation keeps them clear.

246
00:17:21,233 --> 00:17:23,373
Suppose the next turn is M.

247
00:17:23,660 --> 00:17:27,196
Apply the old rotation first, then the new turn.

248
00:17:27,570 --> 00:17:28,998
That gives M times R.

249
00:17:31,114 --> 00:17:33,138
Update the selected cubelets.

250
00:17:33,486 --> 00:17:37,754
Their past moves collapse into one accumulated transformation.

251
00:17:38,103 --> 00:17:40,936
We can forget the sequence and keep its effect.

252
00:17:43,525 --> 00:17:45,636
Our animation moves smoothly.

253
00:17:45,996 --> 00:17:49,328
Rubix stores only the completed quarter turns.

254
00:17:49,687 --> 00:17:55,447
A quarter turn sends each axis to another axis, perhaps reversing its sign.

255
00:17:57,861 --> 00:18:02,675
So the stored matrix entries stay minus one, zero, or one.

256
00:18:03,035 --> 00:18:06,614
No rounding is needed to represent a completed move.

257
00:18:06,974 --> 00:18:11,934
These matrices describe rotations without stretching or reflections.

258
00:18:12,308 --> 00:18:15,015
There are twenty four possible orientations.

259
00:18:18,411 --> 00:18:19,852
There is one final question.

260
00:18:20,201 --> 00:18:21,699
When is a cubelet solved?

261
00:18:23,824 --> 00:18:26,715
When every sticker points in its home direction.

262
00:18:27,107 --> 00:18:31,609
The transformed diagonal matrix must equal the original one.

263
00:18:33,749 --> 00:18:34,991
Remember the column sum?

264
00:18:35,340 --> 00:18:38,500
Equal sticker normals also put the cubelet at home.

265
00:18:38,863 --> 00:18:42,505
The same equality checks facing and location together.

266
00:18:45,105 --> 00:18:47,526
And this condition has a lovely subtlety.

267
00:18:47,875 --> 00:18:49,345
Watch the white center spin.

268
00:18:51,463 --> 00:18:56,554
Its local x and y arrows turn, but the white normal still points up.

269
00:18:56,922 --> 00:19:01,985
Its one sticker is home, so an ordinary cube considers this center solved.

270
00:19:04,119 --> 00:19:10,291
Demanding the identity matrix would ask for extra information the unmarked sticker cannot show.

271
00:19:10,639 --> 00:19:14,382
A picture cube would need that extra orientation constraint.

272
00:19:16,941 --> 00:19:18,660
Return to our opening problem.

273
00:19:19,020 --> 00:19:23,471
How do we teach the computer to turn the cube, including every sticker?

274
00:19:26,101 --> 00:19:29,303
Give each cubelet a home address and a rotation.

275
00:19:29,677 --> 00:19:32,093
The address supplies its sticker normals.

276
00:19:32,468 --> 00:19:36,585
The rotation carries those arrows and locates the cubelet.

277
00:19:36,944 --> 00:19:43,111
Read its current position to select a layer, then apply the same turn to every cubelet in it.

278
00:19:46,131 --> 00:19:50,409
The colors now move correctly because the geometry keeps them attached.

279
00:19:50,737 --> 00:19:57,107
That is the elegant choice in Rubix: a description that makes the relationships we need follow together.

280
00:19:57,504 --> 00:19:59,852
Finding a solution is another challenge.

281
00:20:00,180 --> 00:20:02,746
We have built the world in which that search happens.

282
00:20:06,397 --> 00:20:13,528
This way of seeing matrices was inspired by Grant Sanderson's Essence of Linear Algebra, from Three Blue One

283
00:20:13,528 --> 00:20:14,088
Brown.

284
00:20:16,178 --> 00:20:17,614
It is a wonderful series.

285
00:20:17,933 --> 00:20:22,893
Grant makes the geometry feel discoverable, and the equations feel earned.

286
00:20:23,212 --> 00:20:26,138
If you enjoyed this connection, I warmly recommend it.

287
00:20:28,243 --> 00:20:29,855
Start with a physical question.

288
00:20:30,246 --> 00:20:32,809
Find a description that keeps the geometry.

289
00:20:33,158 --> 00:20:35,636
Let the mathematics reveal what follows.
